  "notes": "Uptempo neotraditional with a female lead — the entry notes \"the catalogue skews *fast* for the era — midtempo 90–119, uptempo 125–156, few ballads,\" so the band starts at 74 but centres at 128, well above the genre's 100. Drums are the stated default: \"a two-beat with quarter kick and a hard backbeat, hat opening on 4,\" which is quarter-note kick anchors with `openHat.pos` on beat 4 at high probability. The bass is quoted and is the record's forward lean: \"staccato quarters or two-beat root-fifth; no sustain,\" hence `followRootsProb` near the ceiling and anticipation near zero. Harmony is \"I–IV–V with V7 and a I–I7–IV blues turn on the uptempo cuts\" — the sevenths live in `extensions` and the blues turn in the major_blues scale weight, because the numeral parser reads degrees only. Melody is \"rhythmically dense and syllabic (lyric-forward), narrow range, with the hook landing on the downbeat of the chorus.\" The form has no bridge on most cuts — \"the solo does that job\" — which is why the arrangement is not authored here and the section grammar is left to the genre. ⚠ Boundary flag from the research: the fastest cuts are train-beat material and that groove belongs to `country-train`. ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"tempo distribution documented; harmony and palette generalised from the lane [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the hat fill density and the countermelody ratio.",
